<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Norodom Ranariddh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1944">1944</a>–<a href="/years/2021">2021</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Norodom Ranariddh was a Cambodian prince and politician, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norodom-of-cambodia">King Norodom</a> Sihanouk and leader of the royalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funcinpec">FUNCINPEC</a> party. After FUNCINPEC won the UN-supervised elections of 1993 he became first prime minister, sharing power with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hun-sen">Hun Sen</a> as second prime minister. Hun Sen ousted him in armed fighting in July 1997, and he later served as president of the National Assembly.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/norodom-ranariddh.jpg" alt="Norodom Ranariddh" width="320" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: His Royal Highness Samdech Krom Preah Norodom Ranariddh · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ranariddh_self_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Norodom Ranariddh was born on 2 January 1944 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phnom-penh">Phnom Penh</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norodom-sihanouk">Norodom Sihanouk</a> and his first wife, Phat Kanhol, a ballet dancer attached to the royal court. When she remarried he was three years old, and he grew up mostly in the care of his aunt Norodom Ketkanya and grandaunt Norodom Sobhana, close to his grandparents but distanced from his father. In 1958 he was sent to a boarding school in Marseille with his half-brother Norodom Chakrapong. He planned to study medicine but was persuaded by his grandmother Sisowath Kossamak to study law, and in 1961 he enrolled at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-paris">University of Paris</a>. He later became a law researcher and lecturer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Ranariddh declined to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funcinpec">FUNCINPEC</a> when Sihanouk formed it in 1981, disagreeing with its association with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a>, but joined in June 1983 and took charge of its diplomatic activities in Asia from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>. He became commander-in-chief of its army in 1986, secretary-general in 1989 and president in February 1992. He signed the Paris Peace Accords in October 1991 as a member of the Supreme National Council.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funcinpec">FUNCINPEC</a> won the 1993 election it formed a coalition with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodian-peoples-party">Cambodian People's Party</a>, and Ranariddh became First Prime Minister with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hun-sen">Hun Sen</a> as Second. Benny Widyono observed that although Ranariddh was nominally senior he held less executive power. Relations deteriorated from early 1996 over the distribution of authority and rival approaches to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a>. In October 1994 he and Hun Sen had dropped Sam Rainsy as Finance Minister, and in May 1995 Ranariddh expelled Rainsy from FUNCINPEC.</p><p>Clashes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funcinpec">FUNCINPEC</a> and CPP troops on 6 July 1997 amounted to his effective ouster. The Foreign Ministry called him a criminal and a traitor and accused him of conspiring with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a>, and Ung Huot replaced him as First Prime Minister in August. Ranariddh returned on 12 November 1998 and was nominated President of the National Assembly on 25 November.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ranariddh resigned as President of the National Assembly on 14 March 2006 and left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. He founded the Norodom Ranariddh Party in November 2006 and was convicted of embezzlement in March 2007, fleeing to Malaysia before his sentence. He was pardoned in September 2008 and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funcinpec">FUNCINPEC</a>, which he led again from January 2015. A car accident during the 2018 election campaign killed his second wife. He died on 28 November 2021 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aix-en-provence">Aix-en-Provence</a>, where he had gone for medical treatment.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Norodom Ranariddh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Norodom Ranariddh</b> and Hun Sen became joint prime ministers of a coalition government.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-cambodia">Kingdom of Cambodia</a></li><li><blockquote>The UN-run elections of May 1993 were won by Prince <b>Norodom Ranariddh's</b> royalist FUNCINPEC party, with the CPP second.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-cambodia">State of Cambodia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phnom-penh">Phnom Penh</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/cambodia">Cambodia</a>. Died in Vauvenargues, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=11.57,104.92&amp;year=1994" aria-label="Phnom Penh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7436 1732 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Norodom Ranariddh"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7886 2002h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7886 2002h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=11.57,104.92&amp;year=1994">What was here in 1994? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
